          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Beginnings in Medical Science</strong><br>\nPaul Ehrlich was born on March 14, 1854, in Strehlen, in what is now Poland. He was a German physician and scientist who studied medicine with a focus on histology, the study of microscopic structures in biological tissues.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Staining Techniques in Microscopy</strong><br>\nEhrlich developed new staining techniques, allowing scientists to see cells and their components under the microscope with unprecedented clarity. His methods are still in use today, with only slight modifications.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Foundation of Chemotherapy</strong><br>\nEhrlich's work laid the foundation for chemotherapy. He pursued the idea of a \"magic bullet,\" a compound that could selectively target disease-causing organisms without harming the host.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine</strong><br>\nIn 1908, Ehrlich received the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for his contributions to immunology. He shared the prize with Russian scientist Elie Metchnikoff for their work on immunity.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Breakthroughs in Treating Syphilis</strong><br>\nEhrlich's research led to the discovery of the first effective treatment for syphilis in 1910. The drug, called Salvarsan, was the result of his extensive search for the magic bullet.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Contributions to Immunology</strong><br>\nEhrlich's theories on immunity and his experimental work became cornerstones of immunology. He proposed the side-chain theory to explain the body's immune response to infection.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
